izabeth Hardwick's writing class at Barnard. After he graduated, he was welcomed into her home as a friend and mentee, and he became close with Hardwick and her best friend, neighbor, and fellow founder of The New York Review of Books, Barbara Epstein. Pinckney found himself at the heart of the New York literary world. He was surrounded by the great writers of the time, like Susan Sontag, Robert Lowell, and Mary McCarthy, as well as the overlapping cultural revolutions and communities that swept New York: the New Wave in film, rock, and writing; the art of Felice Rosser, Jean-Michel Basquiat, Lucy Sante, Howard Brookner, and Nan Goldin; the influence of feminism on American culture and literature; the black arts movement confronted by black feminism; and New Negro veterans experiencing the return of their youth as history. Pinckney filtered the avant-garde life he was exposed to downtown and the radical intellectual tradition of The Review through the moral values he inherited and adapted from abolitionist and Reconstruction black culture.","primaryTopic":"Literary Biography","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Memoir & Autobiography","mappedSubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily recounts Darryl Pinckney's personal experiences and mentorship in the literary world of New York, fitting it firmly under Memoir & Autobiography, while also addressing themes of cultural significance and race.","id":"subject-evidence-book-come-back-in-september-a-literary-education-on-west-sixty-seventh-street-manhattan-darryl-pinckn-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"African American authors","mappedSubject":"Race & Ethnicity","score":6,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-come